reasoning_over_luck.evidence:
The only random element is the coin deciding whether each player's 12 or 24 comes first. Every player receives exactly 12 + 24 = 36 in pots, so chance changes only ordering and information, not endowment. Its share of the outcome is small, roughly under 10%.

Under intended good play, results are decided by:
- calibrating offers to the rejection threat;
- responders inferring the hidden pot from offers and talk (6,6 could mean keep 0 or keep 12);
- inferring the second pot from the first;
- estimating others' private totals from public offers and votes, to decide whether spiteful rejection of a leader pays.

The coin cannot decide the winner when all players play well. Skilled bluffing and inference are clearly favoured over thoughtless play, as shown by the losses of policies (a) to (c).
